Original work. No third-party skill, prompt, or asset is vendored here.

The problem space was surveyed before this was written — see
skills/asgard-office/references/landscape.md for the full list, star counts, and
the reasoning behind each choice. Two entries in that survey carry conditions
worth restating:

  * Anthropic's document skills (anthropics/skills, skills/{docx,pptx,xlsx,pdf})
    are source-available, not open source. Their licence forbids reproduction,
    derivative works, and redistribution. They were read to understand what the
    problem demands and nothing was copied from them; every line here is
    original work built on MIT- and Apache-licensed libraries. This is why the
    lane is a clean-room implementation rather than a vendoring, and why it
    differs in architecture (pure Python with no required system binaries, a
    static gate that runs without a renderer, one placeholder grammar across all
    three formats, a schema-checked template registry).

  * python-docx-template (docxtpl, LGPL-2.1) established the convention of
    Jinja-style placeholders and repeating table rows inside a .docx. That idea
    is reimplemented here, not depended on, so the same grammar works
    identically in .docx, .pptx, and .xlsx.

Runtime dependencies, all permissively licensed and installed from PyPI rather
than vendored:

  python-docx   MIT        Word read/write
  python-pptx   MIT        PowerPoint read/write
  openpyxl      MIT        Excel read/write
  pypdf         BSD-3      PDF read
  PyYAML        MIT        spec front matter

LibreOffice is used opportunistically for rendering and workbook recalculation
when it is present on the machine. It is never bundled, never required, and its
absence is reported rather than worked around.
